00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.270
How did The Other Father get to The Beldam’s
world?

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:54.490
Based on the information available in the
book, there are two possibilities.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.260
One, is that it was actually the initial inhabitant;
it was there before the Beldam discovered

00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.620
the realm and made it into her lair, killing
whatever other creatures dwelled there and

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.810
keeping this one around only to do her bidding.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:08.780
The Other Father is described as a huge, white, swollen
monster, with a vague face like pale clay.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:12.721
It had something that resembled a nose, and
the mouth does not appear when closed, but

00:02:12.721 --> 00:02:15.200
opens up with sticky pale stands when open.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.480
The skin is described similar to warm bread
dough and when Coraline meets it, it has black

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:23.510
button eyes, but those were probably not originally
part of it’s anatomy -- likely added by

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.790
The Other Mother as a sign that she had taken
its soul.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.269
This leads me to the second possibility about
The Other Father’s origins.

00:02:29.269 --> 00:02:31.870
It may have been one of the previous victims
of The Beldam.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.159
When Coraline meets the ghost children their
souls have been separated from their bodies

00:02:35.159 --> 00:02:38.489
and they’re no longer able to remember details
about their true selves.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:42.480
What if after the The Beldam begins feasting
on the souls, the bodies associated with them

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.349
lose their detail and lose what makes them
them?

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.150
There are three ghost children whose souls
have been taken, two girls and one boy.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.260
The Other Mother has three accomplices, two
female and one male.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.799
Remember, Wybie isn’t in the book, and the
man upstairs is actually just a pack of rats

00:02:55.799 --> 00:02:56.930
in human clothing.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.620
So The Other Father may have originally been
the body of the young ghost boy, that was

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:05.159
sucked of its individuality by the Beldam,
and then reformed and reconfigured to look like

00:03:05.159 --> 00:03:06.159
Coraline’s father.

00:03:06.159 --> 00:03:10.060
The idea is backed up when Coraline has this
realization: “These things—even the thing

00:03:10.060 --> 00:03:14.079
in the cellar—were illusions, things made
by the other mother in a ghastly parody of

00:03:14.079 --> 00:03:16.829
the real people
and real things on the other end of the corridor.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:19.550
She could not truly make anything, decided
Coraline.

00:03:19.550 --> 00:03:23.150
She could only twist and copy and distort
things that already existed.”

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.480
When The Jones Family moved to the house,
The Beldam wanted to turn The Other World

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.629
into an ideal paradise for Coraline in order
to trick her into staying, and so, formed

00:03:30.629 --> 00:03:34.780
herself and her prisoners into ideal versions
of the people in Coraline’s life.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.579
The real Mr. Jones, not the one from the Counting
Crows song but Coraline’s dad, makes recipes

00:03:38.579 --> 00:03:42.560
that are too complex for Coraline’s simple
taste buds, but chad The Other Father makes

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.549
her delicious midnight snacks and hot chocolate.

00:03:45.549 --> 00:03:48.989
Coraline first meets her Other Father after
arriving in The Other World, andher Other

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:50.930
Mother tells her to go fetch him for lunch.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:54.180
She finds him in his study with big black
shiny buttons for eyes.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.700
He claims that he’s starving, and that they’ve
been waiting for her for a long time.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:01.349
We know that The Beldam doesn’t thrive on
real food, but rather feasts on childrens’

00:04:01.349 --> 00:04:04.549
souls -- so it’s possible that she made
The Other Father say this line about being

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:07.180
hungry just to add authenticity to the illusion.

00:04:07.180 --> 00:04:10.730
In this analysis, I’m looking at the character
from the book, not the movie -- but the book

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.870
doesn’t tell us whether or not The Other
Father actually eats the food.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:18.910
In the movie he does, whereas The Other Mother
does not, she only eats insects and souls.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.650
“She'd just love something to eat.”

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:24.650
“Eat?

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:25.650
That ridiculous.”

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:29.569
Another possibility is that The Beldam is
unable to suppress her subconscious desires

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:32.319
from manifesting in The Other Father while
she’s controlling him.

00:04:32.319 --> 00:04:35.699
Again, I’m hesitant to bring this up because
it’s only in the movie, not the book, but

00:04:35.699 --> 00:04:39.199
when he sings the song for Coraline he’s
being controlled by these hands created by

00:04:39.199 --> 00:04:40.199
The Other Mother.

00:04:40.199 --> 00:04:41.349
“In Soviet Russia…”

00:04:41.349 --> 00:04:43.449
“This piano plays me.”

00:04:43.449 --> 00:04:48.395
And the lyrics of the song call her a peach,
a doll and “cute as a button”.

00:04:49.045 --> 00:04:52.929
“She’s a peach, she’s a doll, she’s
a pal of mine.

00:04:52.929 --> 00:05:00.110
She’s as cute as a button in the eyes of
everyone who ever laid their eyes on Coraline.”

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:04.860
Calling her a doll and button shows The Beldam’s
desire to take her soul and sew buttons over

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:09.310
her eyes and calling her a peach shows that
The Beldam sees her as a tasty snack.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.402
“Mmm… who’s starving, raise your hand.

00:05:12.402 --> 00:05:13.118
Whoa!

00:05:13.118 --> 00:05:14.154
Ho, hoo!”

00:05:14.165 --> 00:05:18.310
Again, it’s not his hands that rise, it’s
the Beldam’s hands controlling him.

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:21.522
But going back to the book now, the Other
Father remains mostly passive in the coming

00:05:21.522 --> 00:05:22.022
scenes.

00:05:22.022 --> 00:05:25.370
He helps The Other Mother clean up after lunch,
and when Coraline goes outside to explore,

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.460
he tells her they’ll be waiting for her
to come back with smiles and waves.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.800
When she gets back, he is the one to suggest
that if she likes it in their world, she can

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.450
stay forever by letting them sew the button
onto her eyes.

00:05:34.450 --> 00:05:38.900
He tries to assure her that it won’t hurt,
which makes her think that it definitely will.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.690
Coraline goes back to her own world to discover
that her real parents have gone missing, so

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:45.979
she eventually has to return to the Beldam’s
world to try to win them back, but when she

00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:50.097
does, she notices some strange new behaviour
coming from her Other Father.

00:05:59.149 --> 00:06:03.160
When Coraline returns to the alternate world,
she’s greeted by her Other Mother, but her

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:07.069
Other Father is just strangely sitting in
a chair in the hallway before he stands up

00:06:07.069 --> 00:06:08.069
to greet her.

00:06:08.069 --> 00:06:11.039
The Other Mother at least does stuff; she
has things of her own to tend to.

00:06:11.039 --> 00:06:14.590
But The Other Father is almost like an item,
just waiting to be used by her.

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:18.669
But it seems that her ability to manipulate
him is dictated by some kind of energy, because

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:22.669
when Coraline first arrives, everything is
set up perfectly to make him almost indistinguishable

00:06:22.669 --> 00:06:23.870
from her real father.

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:27.460
But as she grows more and more hungry, the
cracks in this facade are starting to show,

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.350
and she’s not able to have full control
over him all of the time.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.639
When Coraline asks why they don’t have their
own key for the door between the two worlds,

00:06:33.639 --> 00:06:34.690
he blurts out an answer.

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.180
“There is only one key.

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:37.900
Only one door,” said the other father.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:39.030
“Hush,” said the other mother.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.470
“You must not bother our darling Coraline’s
head with such

00:06:41.470 --> 00:06:42.470
trivialities.”

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:46.150
This is the first time he says something out
of line, meaning that her control over him

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.310
could be slipping.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:51.011
He gets even weirder the next morning when
Coraline goes into his study looking for the

00:06:51.011 --> 00:06:52.011
Other Mother.

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:56.370
He sat at his desk, not actually doing anything
-- not even pretending to be doing anything.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:07:00.110
She also notices that his face looks more
vague, describing it as looking like “bread

00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:01.719
dough that had begun to rise.”

00:07:01.719 --> 00:07:05.540
He tells her that The Other Mother is outside
fixing the doors, so she tries to engage him

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:06.669
in conversation.

00:07:06.669 --> 00:07:10.100
Instead of the usual friendly attitude he
puts on, he tells her that he’s not allowed

00:07:10.100 --> 00:07:13.750
to talk to her, but he’ll continue to be
hospitable once The Other Mother is back.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:17.630
But when Coraline tells him that she’s going
to go exploring, he accidentally slips up

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:21.000
and tells her there’s no point, because
the Beldam didn’t create anything further

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.020
than the eye can see.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:26.110
“He looked embarrassed and he put one finger
to his lips again, as if he had just said

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.139
too much.”

00:07:27.139 --> 00:07:30.919
He is the only character to demonstrate the
free will to say what he wants, but the Other

00:07:30.919 --> 00:07:35.340
Mother punishes him for exercising this ability
by locking him away in the cellar underneath

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:36.409
the abandoned flat.

00:07:36.409 --> 00:07:40.439
He also continues to lose whatever resemblance
he has left to Coraline’s real father, and

00:07:40.439 --> 00:07:44.419
by the time Coraline finds him down there,
he’s swelled out into his monstrous form,

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:48.539
with only his voice somewhat resembling that
of the original Other Father, and his two

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:51.980
black button eyes, signifying he’s still
under the control of The Beldam.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.729
She would use that control as a trap for Coraline.

00:07:54.729 --> 00:07:58.199
At this point, the creature has had enough,
and is trying to fight back against The Other

00:07:58.199 --> 00:07:58.699
Mother.

00:07:58.699 --> 00:08:02.259
Coraline figures out that he’s been locked
away as punishment for his loose lips earlier,

00:08:02.259 --> 00:08:04.110
and the creature nods to confirm.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:05.499
He also tries to warn her,

00:08:05.499 --> 00:08:06.499
“Run, child.

00:08:06.499 --> 00:08:07.669
Leave this place.

00:08:07.669 --> 00:08:12.110
She wants me to hurt you, to keep you here
forever, so that you can never finish the

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.729
game and she will win.

00:08:13.729 --> 00:08:16.409
She is pushing me so hard to hurt you.

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:17.409
I cannot fight her.”

00:08:17.409 --> 00:08:18.889
The movie is a little bit different.

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:22.069
In the movie, there’s no cellar scene and
The Other Father seems to have been crafted using 

00:08:22.069 --> 00:08:26.259
a pumpkin from the garden, but as time
goes on he loses his father-like qualities

00:08:26.259 --> 00:08:27.620
and becomes more...

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:28.620
Halloween-y.

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:31.520
But the way he tries to fight The Beldam’s
control is very similar.

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:33.190
Roll it.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.710
“Sorry.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:37.750
So sorry.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:41.329
Mother making me.

00:08:41.329 --> 00:08:46.227
Don’t wanna hurt you.

00:08:46.227 --> 00:08:49.277
Take it!”

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:54.710
In the book, when The Other Mother tries to
make it attack her, Coraline encourages it

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:58.529
to be brave, which is interesting, because
from the very beginning, The Other Mother

00:08:58.529 --> 00:09:01.790
has been trying to make this thing into an
ideal version of Coraline’s dad.

00:09:01.790 --> 00:09:05.610
However, it seems that the one thing she didn’t
teach it was bravery, and Coraline’s real dad

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.120
was the one to teach Coraline the concept
of bravery after this one incident where they

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:12.710
accidentally walked into a swarm of wasps,
and he had to stay back and let them sting

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:13.930
him in order for her to get away.

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.600
I guess the message here is that you can dress
someone up and make them appear a certain

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:21.700
way, but you can’t create bravery artificially,
that’s something that you must learn from

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:22.700
experience.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.649
However, the monster was not able to hold
off The Beldam any longer.

00:09:25.649 --> 00:09:29.600
It moved between her and the steps, cutting
off her exit and it gradually grew in size;

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:33.898
it’s voice becoming less and less human,
before finally it lunged across the room towards

00:09:33.898 --> 00:09:34.398
her.

00:09:34.398 --> 00:09:37.471
After seeing the monster looking lost after
one of it’s button eyes had fallen off earlier,

00:09:37.471 --> 00:09:41.780
she ripped off the second eye, causing it
to roar in pain and charge the area that it

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:44.430
had last seen her standing, but it was too
slow.

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:50.089
It’s only option was to go quiet and listen...
and it could hear her tip-toeing up the steps

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:54.690
after her, but she was too quick and it crashed
against the trap door above as she closed

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:57.589
it, perhaps being forever trapped and forgotten.

00:09:57.589 --> 00:10:01.920
Never again do we see the horror that temporarily
masqueraded as The Other Father, leaving both

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.300
its origins and its fate a mystery that I
can really only speculate on as I have in

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:07.005
this video.

00:10:07.005 --> 00:10:11.180
But if not for the creature’s timely rush
of courage, our hero may have never been warned

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:15.380
of The Beldam’s nasty deceptions, and likely
would have been the next victim to be used

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:17.130
and abused by The Other Mother.

